<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.10.2: Working with Quadratic Factored Form with Leading Coefficient Other Than One</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02251</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.10.2: Working with Quadratic Factored Form with Leading Coefficient Other Than One</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c6bb8857-f053-431d-ae57-759456f9659a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Each row in the given tables contains a pair of equivalent expressions. Complete the tables by converting the given expression into <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">standard form</term>. If you get stuck, try drawing a diagram.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">[Remember to use the ^ key to enter exponents. You can find this symbol by pushing shift and the number 6 on your keyboard at the same time.]</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00001">
            <tgroup cols="2">
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><para id="para-00003">\((3x + 1)(x + 4)\)</para></entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Enter the standard form of the equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">\(3x^2+13x +4\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00002">
            <tgroup cols="2">
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><para id="para-00007">\((3x + 2)(x + 2)\)</para></entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Enter the standard form of the equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">\(3x^2 + 8x + 4\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00003">
            <tgroup cols="2">
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><para id="para-00011">\((3x + 4)(x + 1)\)</para></entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Enter the standard form of the equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">\(3x^2 + 7x + 4\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>To find the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">factored form</term> of a <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">quadratic expression</term> with a leading <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00004">coefficient</term> other than 1, answer parts a–d.</item>
      </list>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00004">
        <tgroup cols="2">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> </entry>
              <entry> \(5x^2+21x +4\) </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">This quadratic expression has a leading coefficient of 5. Identify the factors of 5 to determine the leading term of each binomial in factored form.</para>
          <para id="para-00016">\(({\style{color:red}\_}{\style{color:red}\_}{\style{color:red}\_}\;x+\_\_\_)({\style{color:red}\_}{\style{color:red}\_}{\style{color:red}\_}\;x+\_\_\_)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Enter the factors of 5.</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00019">5 and 1; the factored form will have the structure \((5x +\_\_\_)(x + \_\_\_)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00020">The constant term in this quadratic expression is +4. Identify all the factors of +4.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Enter the factors of +4.</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00023">1 and 4 2 and 2</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00024">The <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00005">linear term</term> in the expression is \(21x\). Place each of the factors of +4 you found in part b into the remaining blanks of the factored form structure from part a. Which of these results in a linear term of \(21x\) when you use FOIL? (Keep in mind that the order you place these factors into your factored form structure might affect the outcome, so try both orders if needed.)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00025">Enter your answer.</para>
          <para id="para-00026">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00027">1 and 4; 1 must be placed in the binomial with \(5x\). 4 must be placed in the binomial with \(x\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00028">What is the factored form of \(5x^2+ 21x + 4\)?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00029">Enter the factored form of \(5x^2+ 21x + 4\).</para>
          <para id="para-00030">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00031">\((5x + 1)(x + 4)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00032">Use the strategy you developed in question 4 to find the factored form of the quadratic expression shown here in standard form.</para>
          <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00005">
            <tgroup cols="2">
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> </entry>
                  <entry> \(3x^2 + 7x + 4\) </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00033">Enter the factored form of \(3x^2 + 7x + 4\).</para>
          <para id="para-00034">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00035">\((3x + 4)(x + 1)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
            <para id="para-00036">Find the factored form of the quadratic expression shown.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00006">
              <tgroup cols="2">
                <tbody>
                  <row>
                    <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry> </entry>
                    <entry><para id="para-00037">\(6x^2+ 11x − 10\)</para></entry>
                  </row>
                </tbody>
              </tgroup>
            </table>
            <para id="para-00038">This quadratic expression is different from those in the activity, since its leading coefficient has more than one set of possible factors.</para>
            <para id="para-00039">Use the strategy you learned in the activity to factor the expression. If the pair of factors you selected using the leading coefficient, 6, do not give you the correct solution, begin again with another set of factors.</para>
            <para id="para-00040">The expression is also different since the constant term is negative, -10. This means when you consider the factors of the constant term, one must be positive and one must be negative.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00041">Enter the factored form of \(6x^2+ 11x − 10\).</para>
            <para id="para-00042">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00043">\((3x − 2)(2x + 5)\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00044">Find the factored form of the quadratic expression \(3x^2+ 5x − 2\).</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Working with Expressions of the Form \((px + m)(qx + n)\)</title>
        <para id="para-00045">You have factored and solved many different types of <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00006">quadratic equations</term>, but the leading <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00007">coefficient</term> of the \(x^2\) term was often 1.</para>
        <para id="para-00046">Let’s look at the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00008">factored form</term> of a more complex expression to better understand the process.</para>
        <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00048">Write \((4x + 1)(x + 3)\) in <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00009">standard form</term>.</para>
        <para id="para-00049">To find the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00010">quadratic expression</term> in standard form, use the distributive property of multiplication over addition, or FOIL.</para>
        <para id="para-00050">\(\begin{array}{rcl}\left(4x+1\right)\left(x+3\right)&amp;=&amp; 4x^2+12x+x+3\\&amp;=&amp; 4x^2+13x+3\end{array}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00051">Now, let’s try to go the other direction from standard form to factored form.</para>
        <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00053">Write \(2x^2+ 13x + 15\) in factored form.</para>
        <para id="para-00054">Since the leading coefficient is 2, we know the factored form must have the structure:</para>
        <para id="para-00055">\((2x +\_\_\_)(x + \_\_\_)\)</para>
        <para id="para-00056">Since the constant term is +15, the two missing numbers in the factored form must have a product of 15.</para>
        <para id="para-00057">The possibilities include:</para>
        <para id="para-00058">+1 and +15 +3 and +5 -1 and -15 -3 and -5</para>
        <para id="para-00059">Since the middle term, \(13x\), is positive, we know that only the positive factors need to be considered.</para>
        <para id="para-00060">Since the two factors are already different inside the parentheses, the order in which we place the numbers is important. We will try both, if necessary.</para>
        <para id="para-00061">Let’s try \({\style{color:red}1}\) and \({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}\).</para>
        <para id="para-00062">\(\left(2x+{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}\right)\left(x+{\style{color:red}1}\right)\) \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+2x+15x+15\\&amp;=&amp;2x^2+17x+15\end{array}\) not equivalent to the original expression</para>
        <para id="para-00063">Try reordering the same numbers.</para>
        <para id="para-00064">\(\left(2x+{\style{color:red}1}\right)\left(x+{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}\right)\)\(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+30x+x+15\\&amp;=&amp;2x^2+31x+15\end{array}\) not equivalent to the original expression</para>
        <para id="para-00065">Let’s try \({\style{color:blue}3}\) and \({\style{color:blue}5}\).</para>
        <para id="para-00066">\(\left(2x+{\style{color:blue}5}\right)\left(x+{\style{color:blue}3}\right)\) \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+6x+5x+15\\&amp;=&amp;2x^2+11x+15\end{array}\) not equivalent to the original expression</para>
        <para id="para-00067">Try reordering the same numbers.</para>
        <para id="para-00068">\(\left(2x + {\style{color:blue}3}\right)\left(x + {\style{color:blue}5}\right)\) \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+ 10x + 3x + 15 \\ &amp;=&amp;2x^2+ 13x + 15\end{array}\) \({\style{color:red}\checkmark}\) equivalent to the original expression</para>
        <para id="para-00069">So, the factored form of \(2x^2 + 13x + 15\) is \((2x + 3)(x + 5)\).</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Working with Expressions of the Form \((px + m)(qx + n)\)</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
            <problem id="prob-00010">
              <para id="para-00070">Find the factored form of \(3x^2− 11x − 4\).</para>
              <para id="para-00071">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00010">
              <para id="para-00072">Here is how to find the factored form:</para>
              <para id="para-00073">The factored form will have the structure: \((3x +\_\_\_)(x +\_\_\_)\).</para>
              <para id="para-00074">The factors of -4 are:</para>
              <para id="para-00075">-4 and 1 4 and -1 -2 and 2</para>
              <para id="para-00076">Try -4 and 1.</para>
              <para id="para-00077">\(\left(3x-4\right)\left(x+1\right)\) \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-4x+3x-4\\&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-x-4\end{array}\) not equivalent to the original expression</para>
              <para id="para-00078">Try reordering.</para>
              <para id="para-00079">\(\left(3x+1\right)\left(x-4\right)\) \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-12x+x-4\\&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-11x-4\end{array}\) \({\style{color:red}\checkmark}\)equivalent to the original expression</para>
              <para id="para-00080">The factored form of \(3x^2− 11x − 4\) is \((3x + 1)(x − 4)\).</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>